; SPDX-License-Identifier: AGPL-3.0-or-later
; kitchen.vla - Buffet's own library of template macros (docs/OMAKASE.md, MISE.1): the
; patterns this game writes twice, beside the kit, Alonzo's common.vla, whose verbs it
; never writes again. Both stand in src/ beside the game's source, which includes the
; kit, then this file, each by its own line:
;   (include "common.vla")
;   (include "kitchen.vla")
; and this file includes nothing. The pass resolves every include against the source's
; own folder, an included file's includes too (SPEC.md section 7.8), so an include of
; the kit written here would hold only for a source at one depth.
;
; The library grows by the ratchet (docs/OMAKASE.md, "The service"): a pattern written
; twice in Buffet comes here at its second writing; a pattern a second cartridge writes
; alike goes to Alonzo's kit by a pull request there, the copy, src/common.vla,
; following at the next pin. The rules are the kit's, which are the language's:
; template macros alone, no gensym and no eval; a parameter never the name of a row it
; writes; a verb's pieces are strings, or &expand of strings and of the expand-time
; primitives.
;
; Since POT.1 (2026-10-14): draws, a stream's draws down a column beneath its die.
; Since MENU.1 (2026-10-15): strings, a content sheet from a file of src/content/, and
; picker, CHOOSE over a column of a sheet's rows. Both entered at their first writing,
; where the ratchet waits for a second, since a content file holds its words alone:
; the second content sheet is their second writing.
; Since MENU.2 (2026-10-15), that second writing: strings of several fields, for the
; exam's bank, eight fields wide, the fine print its case of one field, expanding to its
; bytes unchanged; picker unchanged, a picker a field. The same day, at the owner's
; word, the approver and the vintage struck from every row: a row's review is its pull
; request's, and a course is no freeze of the rows before it (BD-1, amended).
; Since MENU.5 (2026-10-15): strings writes no state, and the source writes each content
; sheet's beside its call, hidden but for a sheet a player reads whole, the caveats.
; Since BACK.1 (2026-10-16): as-of and fine-print, a view's date and its fine print, at
; their second writing, Gigs writing them after the Portfolio, which expands over them
; to its bytes unchanged.
; Since POT.10 (2026-10-16): as-of with the day's name and the time to the half hour,
; and bell, a view's line on the market's hours, written by both views at its first
; dish, as draws was by its two streams; and dollars, a view's whole cents spelled with
; two decimals as a text, written by the Portfolio's prices and Gigs' fees at the same
; dish, the owner's word at its hand test: "All dollar cells should show two decimals",
; and padded to end where a number ends at its second.

; ---- the dice (POT.1) ---------------------------------------------------------------
; A stream is a die and its draws. The die is one cell, the stream's state, which the
; source writes: the market's die jumps once a day, and the exam's is a function of the
; seed alone. Its draws stand in the column beneath it, each one step of Park and
; Miller's minimal standard on from the cell above (SPEC.md section 3.5), so a dish that
; needs a draw takes the next one down, and a draw once read never moves. Each draw's
; uniform, the draw over the modulus, a number between 0 and 1, stands beside it in the
; next column, where a formula reads it. Each draw reads the one above it in the same
; frame, so the column is computed a cell at a time, when the die moves and never
; between, which for a few draws a day costs nothing worth timing.

(defmacro (draws sheet first many)
  "a stream's draws, many of them down a column from first, beneath the die in the cell above: each =MOD(above*16807,2147483647), and its uniform beside it in the next column, =draw/2147483647"
  (quasiquote (draws-at (unquote sheet) (unquote (row-expand first)) (unquote (column-expand first)) (unquote many))))

(defmacro (draws-at sheet row column many)
  "draws, its first cell's row and column read: the steps down the column, then the uniforms beside them"
  (formula sheet
    (&expand (address-expand row column) ":" (address-expand (+expand row (-expand many 1)) column))
    (&expand "=MOD(" (address-expand (-expand row 1) column) "*16807,2147483647)"))
  (formula sheet
    (&expand (address-expand row (+expand column 1)) ":" (address-expand (+expand row (-expand many 1)) (+expand column 1)))
    (&expand "=" (address-expand row column) "/2147483647")))

; ---- the strings (MENU.1, MENU.2, MENU.5) -------------------------------------------
; A content sheet holds one kind of string the game shows, a row of fields a row, and a
; file of src/content/ defines it, words alone: one macro named after the file, whose
; body is one call of strings, naming the sheet; then its fields, as a list of each
; field's header and its width in characters, ("disclosure" 100) for a sheet of one
; field; then the rows, each its fields in the list's order. A row names neither its
; reviewer nor its course: its review is the pull request's that adds it, by the
; repository's named reviewer, and a course is a tranche of features, no freeze of the
; rows before it (the owner's words, 2026-10-15; BD-1, amended). The source includes
; the file after its manifest, with every include, and calls its macro where the sheet
; belongs, after a row of its own that gives the sheet's state: hidden, as a workbook
; keeps its reference data at the back, or visible for a sheet a player reads whole, as
; the caveats are (MENU.5). Whether a sheet shows is the game's to say, and the engine
; takes one state a sheet, so strings writes none, and the sheet stands among the tabs
; where the source's row names it. Its table holds the headers in row 1 and a row of
; the call a row of the sheet from row 2, its fields from column A. Beside the table,
; past one empty column, a label beside each value: the rows counted, a value; the row
; shown, which the dish that reads the sheet writes; then a picker a field, the first
; labeled shown, as a sheet of one field labels its only picker, and each later one by
; its field's header, down the column. So the fine print, one field wide, holds its
; text in A, its count, row shown and picker in D1, D2 and D3; the exam's bank, eight
; fields wide, its table in A to H and its values in K1 to K10; and the caveats, four
; fields wide, their table in A to D and their values in G1 to G6. A view reads a picker
; through a reference and never holds its text, since its formula would ride in every
; frame's record. So a row added is a row of the call, and no formula is edited. The
; expander has no length of a list, so strings counts the fields first, a walk that
; hands the count on to the macro that lays the sheet out.

(defmacro (strings content fields & rows)
  "a content sheet, its state the source's: its fields from column A, each a header and a width; a row of the call a row of the sheet from row 2; beside the table, the rows counted, the row shown, which the dish writes, and a picker a field"
  (quasiquote (strings-count (unquote content) (unquote fields) (unquote rows) (unquote fields) 0)))

(defmacro (strings-count content fields rows rest n)
  "strings' first walk: the fields counted, a header and a width each, the count handed on to the sheet"
  (quote-if (null? rest)
    (strings-sheet content fields rows n)
    (strings-count content fields rows (cddr rest) (+expand n 1))))

(defmacro (strings-sheet content fields rows n)
  "the sheet of n fields: its widths, its headers and its labels, then its rows, their count and a picker a field; its state is the source's row, before the call"
  (strings-widths content fields 1)
  (strings-headers content fields 1)
  (labels-from content 1 (+expand n 2) "rows" "the row shown" "shown")
  (strings-labels content (cddr fields) 4 (+expand n 2))
  (strings-from content 2 n fields rows))

(defmacro (strings-widths content fields at)
  "each field's width at its column, from the column given"
  (quote-if (null? fields)
    (begin)
    (begin
      (column content (column-letters-expand at) (car (cdr fields)) shown none)
      (strings-widths content (cddr fields) (+expand at 1)))))

(defmacro (strings-headers content fields at)
  "the headers in row 1: each field's, from the column given"
  (quote-if (null? fields)
    (begin)
    (begin
      (cell content (address-expand 1 at) (car fields))
      (strings-headers content (cddr fields) (+expand at 1)))))

(defmacro (strings-labels content fields at col)
  "each later field's label, its header, down the labels' column from the row given, beside its picker"
  (quote-if (null? fields)
    (begin)
    (begin
      (cell content (address-expand at col) (car fields))
      (strings-labels content (cddr fields) (+expand at 1) col))))

(defmacro (strings-from content row n fields rows)
  "strings' walk over the rows, its list handed on whole: a row of the call a row of the sheet, then the count and a picker a field over the rows written"
  (quote-if (null? rows)
    (begin
      (cell content (address-expand 1 (+expand n 3)) (-expand row 2))
      (strings-pickers content fields 3 1 n (-expand row 1)))
    (begin
      (strings-words content row 1 (car rows))
      (strings-from content (+expand row 1) n fields (cdr rows)))))


(defmacro (strings-words content row col words)
  "one row of the call, a row of the sheet: its fields, each in the next column from the column given"
  (quote-if (null? words)
    (begin)
    (begin
      (cell content (address-expand row col) (car words))
      (strings-words content row (+expand col 1) (cdr words)))))

(defmacro (strings-pickers content fields at k n last)
  "a picker a field, down the values' column from the row given: the kth field's over the kth column, each by the row shown"
  (quote-if (null? fields)
    (begin)
    (begin
      (picker content (address-expand at (+expand n 3)) (address-expand 2 (+expand n 3)) (column-letters-expand k) 2 last)
      (strings-pickers content (cddr fields) (+expand at 1) (+expand k 1) n last))))

; ---- the picker (MENU.1) ------------------------------------------------------------
; CHOOSE over references, the dialect having no INDEX until KERNEL.8 computes one: the
; index, then a reference a row down one column, bare, so the picker stands on the sheet
; whose rows it picks, where it holds the most (MISE.3, probe 3: 1,547 rows below a row
; of headers, within the 8,192 characters a formula may hold). The formula is joined a
; reference a step by a walk that carries the text so far under unquote, so that each
; step hands on a text and never a form, and written once the row passes the last.
; Past 1,547 rows a picker refuses the whole cartridge at its load, and a sheet that
; nears it takes the pages of 500 the owner approved (MENU.1's decision 5).

(defmacro (picker sheet at index letters first last)
  "a picker in one cell: =CHOOSE(index,<letters><first>,...,<letters><last>), its references bare"
  (quasiquote (picker-from (unquote sheet) (unquote at) (unquote (&expand "=CHOOSE(" index)) (unquote letters) (unquote first) (unquote last))))

(defmacro (picker-from sheet at text letters row last)
  "picker's walk: a reference a row joined onto the text so far, then the formula once the row passes the last"
  (quote-if (>expand row last)
    (formula sheet at (&expand text ")"))
    (quasiquote (picker-from (unquote sheet) (unquote at) (unquote (&expand text "," letters row)) (unquote letters) (unquote (+expand row 1)) (unquote last)))))

; ---- the views' frame (BACK.1, POT.10) ----------------------------------------------
; Every view opens on its date and the bell's line and closes on the fine print
; (FRONT.1, MENU.1, POT.10). The Portfolio wrote the date and the fine print first and
; Gigs, the second view, wrote them again, so they came here at their second writing;
; the bell came here at its first dish, both views writing it; each a verb of one formula
; row taking the view's sheet and a cell. The date is the calendar's day's name, its
; year and day of the year, each from 1, and its time to the half hour, unaudited, as
; every number of a view is until the examiner calls (GUARD.3). The bell's line says
; whether the market is open and when it opens or closes: open, a trading day before
; its bell, after the close Monday to Thursday, and from Friday's close to Monday's
; bell; the first two with no joke, being the lines a player acts on, and the bells'
; times written into the texts, which a test holds to the Day sheet's settings. The fine
; print is the Disclosures sheet's picker, a disclosure a month, read through a
; reference, so that no view holds its text in a formula.

(defmacro (as-of sheet at)
  "a view's date in one cell, from the Day sheet's day's name, year, day of the year and time to the half hour: As of Monday, year 1, day 1, 9:30 (unaudited)"
  (formula sheet at "=\"As of \"&Day!$B$18&\", year \"&Day!$B$6&\", day \"&Day!$B$7&\", \"&Day!$B$16&\" (unaudited)\""))

(defmacro (bell sheet at)
  "a view's line on the market's hours in one cell, from the Day sheet: open until the closing bell, opening at the bell, closed for the night, or closed for the weekend"
  (formula sheet at "=IF(Day!$B$19=1,\"The market is open until 16:00.\",IF(AND(Day!$B$17<Day!$B$12,Day!$B$20<Day!$B$3),\"The market opens at 9:30.\",IF(AND(Day!$B$17<Day!$B$12-1,Day!$B$20=Day!$B$3),\"The market has closed. Analysts are analyzing the aftermath.\",\"The market is closed until Monday at 9:30.\")))"))

(defmacro (fine-print sheet at)
  "a view's fine print in one cell: the Disclosures sheet's disclosure of the month, read through a reference"
  (formula sheet at "=Disclosures!$D$3"))

; ---- a view's dollars (POT.10) ------------------------------------------------------
; The record spells a number at its shortest, $1.00 as 1 and $12.30 as 12.3, and holds
; no number format (vla-lang 0.9.4: General and Text), so a view's dollars are a text:
; the dollars, a point and the cents in two digits, from whole cents that are never
; negative, a price or a fee. A text is drawn from its cell's left and a number to its
; right, so each is padded on the left to end where a number ends, in a column 12
; characters wide, as every column of a view's dollars is: its spaces and twice its
; dollars' digits make 16, and none is padded from eight digits. A space is half a
; digit's width in the grid's font to within a sixtieth (system-ui, Segoe UI here, at
; 13 pixels: a digit 7.008, a space 3.561, the point 2.818), so a column's points line
; up, and each text ends within two device pixels short of a number's edge at 150% and
; never past it from 100% to 200% (the owner's word at POT.10's second hand test). A
; sum or a comparison reads the model's cents and never this text. When the record
; holds a number format, this verb becomes a format.

(defmacro (dollars sheet at cents)
  "a view's dollars in one cell or a fill: whole cents, never negative, spelled as a text with two decimals and padded to end where a number ends in a column 12 characters wide"
  (formula sheet at (&expand "=IF(" cents "<1000,\"              \",IF(" cents "<10000,\"            \",IF(" cents "<100000,\"          \",IF(" cents "<1000000,\"        \",IF(" cents "<10000000,\"      \",IF(" cents "<100000000,\"    \",IF(" cents "<1000000000,\"  \",\"\")))))))&INT(" cents "/100)&\".\"&IF(MOD(" cents ",100)<10,\"0\",\"\")&MOD(" cents ",100)")))
